  "query_preview": "..\nThursday and Friday: Upper-level ridging pushing north from the\nDesert Southwest will initiate a warming trend Thursday and \nFriday, driving afternoon high temperatures back into the upper \n80s to mid 90s. Dry air will remain in place with afternoon\nhumidity falling into the teens to low 20s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: Saturday continues to be the primary focus\nof the next seven days. A strong upper-level low will swing \ninland over British Columbia, driving a strong dry cold front \nthrough the Pacific Northwest and positioning an impressive \nupper-level jet (120+ kts at 200 mb) directly over the area. \nModel guidance maintains high surface wind forecasts Saturday\nafternoon and evening. Probabilities for wind gusts exceeding \n40 mph range from 70 to 100 percent across the Columbia Basin, \nWaterville Plateau, Blue Mountains, and Lewiston- Clarkston \nValley. Over the Waterville Plateau and Blue Mountains where \nthe strongest winds will be focused, the National Blend of \nModels indicates a 60 to 80 percent chance of gusts exceeding \n50 mph. These impressive winds combined with minimum RH values \nin the upper teens to low 20s will bring widespread critical to\nlocalized extreme fire weather conditions.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, a sharp cooldown will limit highs\non Sunday to the mid 70s to low 80s, around 10 degrees cooler \nthan on Saturday. West winds will decrease some compared to \nSaturday but will remain breezy, with sustained speeds of 10 to \n20 mph and gusts of 25 to 35 mph. \n\nMonday and Tuesday: A ridge of high pressure moves behind the\nlow pressure system on Monday and Tuesday, bringing \ntemperatures back into the 80s to low 90s with light winds \nforecast across the region. /Fewkes",
